TEST 3116 -- BGPv4 - Unicast Traffic
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-30 18:08-18:18 NZDT, tb470, bench-runner (queue CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md
        row 11). Hand-driven: no framework script.
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20 (stack and IE520-sa)
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4, master member 3), router MAC
        0000.cd37.0d6f. BGP peer: the standalone IE520-sa (84e3.2787.0780).
Case:   AWPTCM-T3116 (ck.db), objective "Check and verify BGPv4 for correct status and
        functionality". One step: "Run background unicast traffic in the relevant scenario.
        See that traffic passes through the scenario. Line rate traffic is achieved as expected."
        The case gives no network diagram. The scenario here: an eBGP IPv4 peering, each router
        advertising its host subnet, and traffic routed through both routers over the
        BGP-learned routes.

I-19 (CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-24.md): the 09-22 blocker ("harness peaks at ~26 Mbps",
  ipv6-2026-09-22/3116.log, SKIPPED 09-23) NO LONGER HOLDS. tcpreplay on tb470 drove
  970-975 Mbps per NIC, in both directions at once, in this run. The case was run on
  Terrence's 2026-09-30 case list and run order.

VERDICT: PASS.
  The eBGP session came up and stayed Established throughout, with no reset. Each router
  installed the other's prefix as a BGP route (`B ... [20/0]`), and the stack programmed it
  into hardware on all three members. Routed unicast then ran at line rate in both
  directions at once:
    1514 B, 30 s: stack->SA 2,404,501/2,404,501 at 970.8 Mbps; SA->stack 2,415,001/2,415,001
    at 974.9 Mbps, 100.0000 % each (98.6-99.0 % of the 81,274 pps wire maximum, which is
    the generator's own ceiling), 0 kernel drops at either receiver.
  Controls:
    - With no BGP, 0 of ~810 K frames each way were delivered.
    - Withdrawing the SA's prefix stopped the stack->SA flow (0/803,001), while the
      still-advertised SA->stack flow stayed at 100 %.
  NOT MEASURED (bench limit, not required by the case): line rate for SMALL frames.
    tcpreplay tops out at 170-229 K pps with 64 B pcap frames (68 B on the wire),
    ~11-15 % of the 1.488 Mpps small-frame rate. All of it was delivered (100 % both ways).

TOPOLOGY (standing bench, no recable; all addressing scratch)
  eth1 (tb470) -- stack port3.0.13  vlan3161 10.116.1.1/24  [host 10.116.1.2 = fake MAC 0231.1600.0001]
  stack port4.0.26 (member 4, 1000BASE-SX) -- SA port1.0.26  vlan3162 10.116.2.1/30 <-> .2/30  (eBGP)
  SA port1.0.2 -- eth2 (tb470)              vlan3163 10.116.3.1/24  [host 10.116.3.2 = fake MAC 0231.1600.0003]
  Stack AS 65116 advertises 10.116.1.0/24; SA AS 65117 advertises 10.116.3.0/24.
  The hosts are static ARP entries with FAKE MACs: tb470 has ip_forward=1, so a frame to a real
  tb470 NIC MAC with a foreign destination would be routed onward by the host. The receivers
  count in promiscuous mode. The SX link's baseline VLAN 4000 and the vlan1 membership of
  port3.0.13 and SA port1.0.2 were changed for the case and restored after it.

